"None of this is something to self-diagnose from a reading passage. What matters here is the logic: Chinese medicine rarely stops at a single, flat label like \"weak qi.\" It keeps asking a more specific question — weak in which direction — because the answer changes what actually helps.": "ab89b28d1f9a",
"She described precisely what had happened.": "fcad6cc0df2c",
"The first unit of this course ended with qi deficiency — a state where the body's basic functions are not being carried out with full force. Once yin and yang enter the picture, qi deficiency can be described more precisely, because it can lean in one of two directions. When the \"warming\" and \"moving\" side of qi runs low, the result is called yang deficiency: a person feels cold, especially in the hands and feet, moves slowly, and often prefers warm drinks and warm weather. When the \"cooling\" and \"moistening\" side runs low instead, the result is called yin deficiency: a person may feel warm in the afternoon or evening, have a dry mouth, and sleep restlessly despite feeling tired.": "687fbf814053",
